{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000001","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Agent Supervision","description":"Guidelines for supervising and coordinating sub-agents in Minerva.","steps":"1. Before spawning a worker agent, decompose the task into clear, self-contained work items.\n2. Create docket items for each piece of work (docket_create type=work_item).\n3. Set clear success criteria in the description.\n4. Spawn workers with minerva_spawn_worker, referencing the docket item ID in the task.\n5. Monitor progress via docket signals — DocketManager emits item_transitioned when workers update status.\n6. When a worker completes, review their output before marking the docket item done.\n7. If a worker gets stuck, check their chat history (minerva_get_chat_history) and provide guidance via minerva_send_message.\n8. Use docket_quality to rate knowledge items that helped or hindered the work.","preconditions":"DocketManager loaded, at least one project docket open.","outcome":"Work is decomposed, tracked, and completed through coordinated sub-agents with full docket traceability.","tags":"agent,supervision,workflow","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000002","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Knowledge Management","description":"How to use docket hints, insights, and quality scoring to build persistent knowledge.","steps":"1. Before starting work on a component, check for existing knowledge: docket_hint_query(component=<name>).\n2. If hints exist, read them and apply. Bump useful hints with docket_quality(score=+3).\n3. During work, when you discover something non-obvious (a gotcha, working command, selector, pattern), save it immediately: docket_hint_set(component, key, value, tags).\n4. If you discover a mental model correction (you assumed X but Y is true), create an insight: docket_create(type=insight, assumed=X, corrected=Y).\n5. If knowledge caused errors or was outdated, downgrade it: docket_quality(score=-3, reason='outdated').\n6. Keep hint values short and actionable — one-liners, small JSON, exact commands.\n7. Use component names that match the system being worked on (e.g., 'godot', 'docket', 'cobrowser').","preconditions":"Docket tools available.","outcome":"Knowledge accumulates across sessions. Future agents benefit from past discoveries without re-learning.","tags":"knowledge,hints,insights,quality","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000003","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Tool Usage Patterns","description":"Efficient patterns for using Minerva's MCP tools.","steps":"1. Start by understanding what's available: minerva_tool_search to find tools by keyword.\n2. For file work: minerva_file_glob to find files, minerva_file_grep to search content, minerva_file_read for targeted reads.\n3. For terminal work: minerva_terminal_create, then minerva_terminal_write with \\r for Enter (not \\n).\n4. For spreadsheets: minerva_create_spreadsheet_editor, then minerva_update_spreadsheet_data for bulk operations.\n5. For webviews: minerva_create_webview_panel with HTML, then minerva_update_webview_panel to iterate.\n6. For notes: minerva_create_note for persistent context, minerva_link_note_to_chat for chat-specific notes.\n7. Prefer targeted queries over broad fetches. If a result is truncated, narrow your query.\n8. Complete tasks in as few tool calls as possible while being thorough.","preconditions":"Minerva MCP tools connected.","outcome":"Efficient tool usage with minimal wasted calls.","tags":"tools,efficiency,patterns","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
{"_type":"item","id":"019d5c00000000000000000000000004","type":"skill","status":"active","title":"Docket Work Tracking","description":"How to use docket for tracking and managing work items.","steps":"1. Create work items with clear titles and descriptions: docket_create(type=work_item, title=..., description=...).\n2. Use parent fields to build hierarchies: docket_create(parent=<parent_id>).\n3. Follow state machines — work_item: backlog → open → in_progress → done.\n4. Use docket_transition to move items through states. Include a note explaining why.\n5. Use docket_query to find items by type, status, tags, or text search.\n6. Use docket_comment to add discussion to items.\n7. Use docket_link to create relationships (blocks, caused_by, follow_up).\n8. For bugs: type=bug, states: new → triaged → active → resolved → verified → closed.\n9. For design changes: type=dcr, states: proposed → approved → designing → implementing → reviewing → shipped.","preconditions":"At least one project docket loaded.","outcome":"Work is tracked with full history, relationships, and state machine enforcement.","tags":"docket,work-tracking,state-machine","created_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00","updated_at":"2026-04-05T03:00:00"}
